# Outstanding Items

Roughly in priority order.

## 1. Asymmetric confidence threshold (top priority)

`CONF_THRESHOLD` in `deployment/inspection_core.py` currently applies
symmetrically to both classes (0.4). A missed defect (false negative) is
more costly than a false alarm, so `FAIL`/defective should trigger at a
**lower** confidence bar than confirming `PASS`. This is a config/logic
change to `run_inspection()`, not a retraining requirement, and should be
validated against the same known-board set already in use — now covering
both connector colors.

## 2. `SAVE_ALL_IMAGES` during threshold tuning

`inspection_core.py` has a `SAVE_ALL_IMAGES` flag, currently `False`. Flip
it to `True` while tuning thresholds so every inspection — not just
non-PASS results — leaves a visual record. This matters because the
existing false-negative case(s) from live validation have no saved image;
the only record is the CSV row. Revert to `False` once tuning is done to
avoid unbounded storage growth.

## 3. Live re-validation of the v6 multi-color fine-tune

The v6 offline test metrics (per-color, per-class AP50) look strong, but
this project has twice now had strong offline numbers hide a real problem
(the v3 leakage incident, and v4's passing-class softening only surfacing
as a live false negative). Before trusting v6 in production:

- Re-run live validation with real boards of **both colors**, at the
  **normal** `CONF_THRESHOLD` (not a lowered diagnostic value).
- Apply a concrete, pre-committed decision rule (e.g. "no more than N false
  PASSes on known-defective boards, no more than M false FLAGs on
  known-passing boards") rather than judging the result after the fact.
- If it doesn't clear that bar, that's a signal the ~75-100 images per
  white class genuinely wasn't enough — not a reason to lower the
  threshold again as a workaround.

## 4. Collect more live-condition defective imagery

Continue collecting images of defective boards under actual deployment
camera/lighting conditions (not just original studio/training images) for
a future retraining iteration, to close the gap between training
conditions and live conditions.

## 5. Recurring re-validation cadence

Establish a fixed known-board test set (now covering both connector
colors) and re-run it periodically, before trusting any future threshold
or model change — not just once after each change.

## 6. Storage retention policy

`logs/flagged_images/` currently accumulates indefinitely with no cleanup
policy. Decide a retention window or archival approach before this becomes
a disk-space problem on the Pi.

## 7. Interim operating posture

Continue running with human spot-checking during the initial production
trial. Do not treat the system as fully autonomous yet — specifically
because of the outstanding false-negative risk (item 1) and the
not-yet-live-validated multi-color fine-tune (item 3).

## Notes for future fixture or connector-variant changes

Before any future physical change (new fixture, new connector color/model,
new lighting), decide explicitly whether the new condition **replaces**
the old one or **coexists** with it — this determines whether to fine-tune
on new data alone or combine it with existing data. See
`training/README.md` for the reasoning and the recurring bug patterns to
watch for either way.
